  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "Changes since 2.1.0\n\n2.2.3\n- A widget added to a smart stack takes the stack's width from the start, instead of running past it until it is next moved.\n- A widget removed while it is still starting no longer leaves its preview behind on the taskbar.\n\n2.2.2\n- New TaskbarButtonResources: the hover and pressed plate of the Windows 11 taskbar buttons, as resources to merge in App.xaml or as brushes for UI built in code (CreateBackgroundBrush, CreateBorderBrush).\n- The whole-preview hover matches the taskbar in both themes and dims while pressed.\n\n2.2.1\n- A flyout always opens at the size WidBar gives it, also on a display at another scale.\n- A flyout can no longer open twice.\n- A flyout that ends up behind other windows comes back on top.\n- Clicks and flyouts respond faster while your widget is busy.\n\n2.2.0\n- The flyout slides out of the taskbar like the Windows 11 quick settings, and stays smooth while your widget is busy.\n- Large flyouts no longer pause or flash on their first open, and settings windows no longer flash black while resized.\n\n2.1.2\n- A widget no longer crashes when WidBar closes.\n- The preview fills the whole height of the taskbar.\n- New HighlightPreviewOnHover, for previews made of separate buttons.\n\n2.1.1\n- Switching Windows between light and dark no longer crashes widgets.\n- Built against Windows App SDK 2.5.1.",
